speechtext:
Of what consequence is it? Was it not bad enough before,
when the home industries were shut down and men were out of work, without having an increase of 104 per cent in the importation of these goods? What harm is it?-the hon. member wants to know. No harm at all; of course not. It is of no consequence, of course, that Canadian workers go without work and that Canadian consumers are to-day paying higher prices than before. I should have thought that there was great harm in all this;
I should have thought that my hon. friend, when he was considering the question of reducing duties, would have thought it advisable at least to reduce the duties as they would affect the output of such companies as could well afford to stand the reduction and still keep going. My hon. friend well knows that since his last budget was brought down some of these mills have had to cease entirely. Foi example, is it a matter of no concern that the York Knitting Mills should have stopped operations?

Amendment agreed to.
Custome tariff-All sugar above number sixteen Dutch standard in colour, and all refined sugars of whatever kinds, grades or standards, not covered by tariff item No. 135, when not exceeding eighty-eight degrees of polarization, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 83 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.50; general tariff, $1.50;
when exceeding eighty-eight degrees but not exceeding eighty-nine degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff. 85 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.53; general tariff, $1.53.
when exceeding eighty-nine degrees but not exceeding ninety degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff. 87 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.55; general tariff, $1.55;
when exceeding ninety degrees but not exceeding ninety-one degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 89 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.58; general tariff, $1.58.
when exceeding ninety-one degrees but not exceeding ninety-two degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 91 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.62; general tariff, $1.62.
when exceeding ninety-two degrees but not exceeding ninety-three degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 93 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.65; general tariff, $1.65.
when exceeding ninety-three degrees but not exceeding ninety-four degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 95 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.68; general tariff, $1.68.
when exeeding ninety-four degrees but not exceeding ninety-five degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, 97 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.70; general tariff, $1.70.
when exceeding ninety-five degrees but not exceeding ninety-six degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff. 99 cents; intermediate tariff, $1.74; general tariff, $1.74;
when exceeding ninety-six degrees but not exceeding ninety-seven degrees, per one hundred pounds; British preferential tariff, $1.01; intermediate tariff, $1,77; general tariff, $1.77;
when exceeding ninety-seven degrees, but not exceeding ninety-eight degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, $1.03; intermediate tariff, $1.80; general tariff, $1.80.
when exceeding ninety-eight degrees, but not exceeding ninety-nine degrees, per one hundred pounds: British preferential tariff, $1.09; intermediate tariff, $1.89; general tariff, $1.89;
Provided that refined sugar shall be entitled to entry under the British preferential tariff upon evidence satisfactory to the Minister of Customs and Excise, that such refined sugar has been manufactured wholly from raw sugar produced in the British colonies and possessions, and not otherwise.
Provided further that sugar imported under this item shall not be subject to special duty when the home consumption value at the place of shipment direct to Canada is more than eight cents per pound.

Item agreed to.
Customs tariff-Liquorice in rolls and sticks, not sweetened:	British preferential tariff, 15 per cent;
Intermediate tariff, 20 per cent; General tariff, 22* per cent.
